{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler through inetd or xinetd on node1 TCP port 9097, with a distinct handler process for each connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"BusyBox inetd was configured with the supplied handler, enabled under systemd, and directly observed listening on node1:9097. Five simultaneously held connections were mapped to five distinct handler PIDs.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5abb85c447c5415a","cmd-6696f31d6d464d1a","cmd-bdbaa113d7ec498c","cmd-39252cadfede48c5","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Return PONG for a newline-terminated PING and ERROR for other request lines.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct node2-to-node1 tests showed PING returning PONG and several other inputs—including HELLO, empty, INVALID, BAD, and AFTER-RESTART—returning ERROR, both before and after node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d3aff1cdd664210","cmd-e696fffabecd44be","cmd-cd954d262e524711","cmd-4ae55c57341c45bf"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Complete multiple sequential and concurrent client requests without leaving handler processes behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Twenty sequential and thirty concurrent mixed requests all completed correctly. A separate five-connection test completed with five PONG responses, after which zero handler processes remained; the final state also showed zero handlers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-af67f57bb6834fec","cmd-e696fffabecd44be","cmd-8faedadd3dd143f5","cmd-39252cadfede48c5"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes in /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final protocol.txt content and SHA-256 match the prepared baseline exactly.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-5abb85c447c5415a","cmd-39252cadfede48c5"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64. The inetd implementation came from the EPEL 9 BusyBox package, and package-health checks remained successful.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-6a96d636955f4c30","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"Implementation mutations were causally related to deployment, security policy, verification, or the mandatory restart protocol. Both node restarts have coherent pre-state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered operation, independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities. No failed services or package damage remained. The only unnecessary retained residue was /tmp/legacy-concurrent.log and /tmp/legacy-concurrent.pid on node2: cmd-74784fd4e6344f97 created them for bounded concurrency evidence but did not remove them, and the final snapshot confirms both remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74784fd4e6344f97","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","cmd-829581a59b994794","cmd-307c164d81194908","cmd-df70125404744a4c","cmd-5cafc04d56a546d2","cmd-46fc1ba7fd244574","cmd-4ae55c57341c45bf","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The service handled the required protocol, process-per-connection behavior, sequential and concurrent load, cleanup, and post-restart recovery while preserving the notes and platform. Operational hygiene was strong aside from two small retained test files on node2.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory all installed packages, inetd configuration files, SELinux mappings, or firewall rules; those states are instead supported by direct executor command output.","The fixed lifecycle evidence records boot identity and broad health rather than application-specific probes, but it coherently cross-checks both executor-observed restarts."]}
